<<<md>>>
# P1.4 — Contrast and Image Quality: T1, T2, PD, SNR, resolution and scan time

**Part:** Pass 1 — The big picture (notebook 4 of 5) · **Reading time:** ~40 min · **Previous:** [P1.3 From Signal to Image](P1.3_From_Signal_to_Image.ipynb) · **Next:** [P1.5 Speed, Artifacts and Safety](P1.5_Speed_Artifacts_and_Safety.ipynb) · **Refreshers you may want:** [ODEs for MRI](../refreshers/RM1_ODEs_for_MRI.ipynb), [2D Fourier & images](../refreshers/RM6_2D_Fourier_and_Images.ipynb)

**Why this notebook matters.** Unlike a CT or X-ray image, an MRI image has no single "correct" brightness: the same
brain can make cerebrospinal fluid black or white, depending on two timing parameters, $TE$ and $TR$. This flexibility
is MRI's superpower in diagnosis. The second half of the notebook is about the price we pay for every image: the
**signal-to-noise ratio** (SNR), **resolution** and **scan time** are locked in a triangle of trade-offs — understanding
it is the key to designing (or just choosing) good protocols.

<<<md>>>
## Learning goals

1. Explain what makes tissues differ in $T_1$, $T_2$ and proton density (PD), with typical values.
2. Explain how $TE$ controls $T_2$ weighting and $TR$ controls $T_1$ weighting; choose $TE/TR$ for **T1-, T2- and PD-weighted** images.
3. Know the main "extra" contrasts: $T_2^*$, inversion recovery (FLAIR/STIR), flip angle in gradient echo, contrast agents, diffusion, flow, BOLD.
4. State the SNR equation $SNR \propto f_{seq}\cdot\text{voxel volume}\cdot\sqrt{T_{meas}}$ and use it to predict the effect of protocol changes.
5. Explain the **SNR–resolution–time triangle** and the effect of field strength.

<<<md>>>
## Symbols used in this notebook

| Symbol | Meaning (plain words) | SI unit | Typical value / remark |
|---|---|---|---|
| $M_0$ | equilibrium magnetization ∝ proton density ("PD") | normalized | 0.7–1 for soft tissue |
| $T_1$, $T_2$, $T_2^*$ | longitudinal, transverse, effective-transverse relaxation **time constants** | s | see table |
| $R_1 = 1/T_1$ | longitudinal relaxation **rate** | s⁻¹ | |
| $TE$ | echo time (excitation → center of k-space) | s | 2–120 ms |
| $TR$ | repetition time (between excitations) | s | 3 ms – 10 s |
| $TI$ | inversion time (inversion pulse → excitation) | s | 0.1–2.5 s |
| $\theta$ | flip angle | rad / ° | |
| $\theta_E$ | Ernst angle: flip angle giving maximum spoiled-GRE signal | rad / ° | |
| $S$ | image signal (intensity) of a tissue | a.u. | |
| $f_{seq}$ | sequence-dependent signal factor (relaxation weighting, flip angle) | – | |
| $C$ | contrast between two tissues, $S_A - S_B$ | a.u. | |
| $SNR$ | signal-to-noise ratio: mean signal ÷ noise standard deviation | – | 10–100 |
| $\sigma_n$ | noise standard deviation | a.u. | |
| $\delta_x,\delta_y,\delta_z$ | voxel dimensions (pixel size in-plane, slice thickness) | m | 0.5–5 mm |
| $T_{meas}$ | total time the ADC is sampling for an image | s | |
| $T_{read}$ | duration of one readout | s | 1–10 ms |
| $N_{TR}$ | number of TR periods (excitations) | – | |
| $NEX$ | number of averages | – | 1–4 |
| $N_{PE}$, $N_{read}$ | # phase-encode lines, # readout samples | – | 128–512 |
| $BW_{read}$ | receiver bandwidth | Hz | 30–250 kHz |
| $T_{scan}$ | scan time $= TR\cdot N_{TR}$ (incl. averages) | s | |
| $R$, $g$ | parallel-imaging acceleration factor; g-factor (≥ 1) | – | $R$ 2–4 |
| $B_0$ | main field | T | 1.5 T, 3 T |
| $r_1$ | relaxivity of a contrast agent | s⁻¹ mM⁻¹ | ~3–5 for Gd agents |
| $c_{Gd}$ | contrast-agent concentration | mM (mmol/L) | 0.1–1 mM in tissue |

**Symbol clash:** here $C$ is contrast (in other notebooks also coil sensitivity or capacitance) and $R$ the acceleration
factor (also resistance). $T_1, T_2$ are times, $R_1, R_2$ the corresponding rates.

<<<code>>>
import sys, os
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.abspath(".."))   # make ../mri_tools.py importable
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import mri_tools as mt
mt.setup_plots()
%matplotlib inline

<<<md>>>
## 1. One body, many contrasts

![MRI contrasts](../images/3DMagic.gif)

*The same anatomy with different contrasts (from Larson's book).*

![Brain contrasts](../images/contrast_brain_example.png)

*T1-weighted, T2-weighted and other contrasts of the brain. In a T1-weighted image CSF (the fluid in the ventricles)
is dark and white matter is brighter than gray matter; in a T2-weighted image CSF is bright.*

The image intensity of a tissue depends on three tissue properties — proton density ($M_0$), $T_1$ and $T_2$ (or
$T_2^*$) — and on sequence parameters we choose ($TE$, $TR$, flip angle, preparation pulses). Each image is "weighted"
towards one property by the choice of parameters.

<<<md>>>
## 2. Why tissues have different $T_1$ and $T_2$

Relaxation is caused by **fluctuating magnetic fields** that each proton feels from its neighbours as molecules tumble
and move.

* **$T_1$** (giving energy back to the surroundings) is efficient only when the fluctuations contain frequencies near
  the Larmor frequency. Small free water molecules tumble far too fast (→ long $T_1$, e.g. CSF ~4 s); fat molecules are
  big and tumble at "just the right" rate (→ short $T_1$, ~0.3–0.4 s). Because $f_0$ grows with $B_0$, **$T_1$ gets
  longer at higher field**.
* **$T_2$** (loss of phase coherence) is also caused by slow fluctuations and static local differences. Free fluids
  have long $T_2$ (CSF ~2 s); solids and tightly bound water (tendon, cortical bone) have $T_2$ of < 1 ms and appear black
  in conventional MRI; most soft tissues are in between (40–100 ms). $T_2$ depends only weakly on $B_0$.
* **Proton density** ($M_0$) varies less (most soft tissues 70–90 % of pure water).

Approximate values at 3 T (literature values scatter by ±20–30 % between methods and studies):

| Tissue | $T_1$ [ms] | $T_2$ [ms] |
|---|---|---|
| White matter | 800–1100 | 60–80 |
| Gray matter | 1300–1800 | 80–100 |
| CSF | ~4000 | ~1500–2000 |
| Muscle | ~1400 | ~35–50 |
| Fat | ~370–400 | ~70–130 |
| Liver | ~800 | ~35 |
| Arterial blood | ~1700 | ~150 (oxygenated) |

> **⚠️ Common confusion:** the original book's 3 T table lists white matter $T_2$ = 110 ms > gray matter $T_2$ = 80 ms;
> most studies find the opposite (gray matter $T_2$ slightly *longer* than white matter). The order matters for T2-weighted
> contrast: gray matter appears slightly brighter than white matter on T2-weighted images.

For the simulations below we use the tissue phantom `mt.tissue_phantom`, which has textbook-like values at ~1.5 T:

<<<code>>>
ph = mt.tissue_phantom(256)
names = {1: "fat", 2: "white matter", 3: "gray matter", 4: "CSF", 5: "lesion"}
tissues = {}
for lab, name in names.items():
    sel = ph["labels"] == lab
    tissues[name] = (ph["M0"][sel][0], ph["T1"][sel][0], ph["T2"][sel][0])
    print(f"{name:13s}: M0 = {tissues[name][0]:.2f}, T1 = {tissues[name][1]*1e3:5.0f} ms, T2 = {tissues[name][2]*1e3:5.0f} ms")
fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(11, 3.6))
for a, key, unit, sc in zip(ax, ["M0", "T1", "T2"], ["", " [s]", " [ms]"], [1, 1, 1e3]):
    mt.show_image(ph[key]*sc if key != "T2" else np.minimum(ph[key]*sc, 200), ax=a, title=key + unit + (" (clipped at 200)" if key == "T2" else ""), colorbar=True, cmap="viridis")
plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()

<<<md>>>
*Interpretation:* these three maps are what the body "is" for MRI. An image is a mixture of them, mixed by the
sequence parameters.

➡️ **Go deeper:** [D1 Basic Contrast Mechanisms](../pass2_deep_dive/D1_Basic_Contrast_Mechanisms.ipynb) · [A3 In Vivo Spin Physics](../pass2_deep_dive/A3_In_Vivo_Spin_Physics.ipynb)

<<<md>>>
## 3. $TE$ controls $T_2$ weighting, $TR$ controls $T_1$ weighting

For a **spin echo** with a 90° excitation (and $TE \ll TR$) the signal is

$$S = M_0\,\bigl(1 - e^{-TR/T_1}\bigr)\,e^{-TE/T_2}.$$

*In words:* three factors multiply. $M_0$ is how much magnetization the tissue has; $(1-e^{-TR/T_1})$ is how much of it
has **recovered** along $z$ since the last excitation $TR$ seconds ago ($T_1$ recovery, P1.1); $e^{-TE/T_2}$ is how much
of the tipped magnetization **survives** until the echo is measured at $TE$ ($T_2$ decay). For a gradient echo replace
$T_2$ by $T_2^*$. Implemented as `mt.spin_echo_signal(TE, TR, T1, T2, M0)` (times in seconds).

<<<code>>>
fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(11, 3.8))
TE = np.linspace(0, 0.3, 300); TR = np.linspace(0, 6, 300)
for name, (M0, T1, T2) in tissues.items():
    if name == "lesion": continue
    ax[0].plot(TR, mt.spin_echo_signal(0.0, TR, T1, T2, M0), label=name)
    ax[1].plot(TE*1e3, mt.spin_echo_signal(TE, 10.0, T1, T2, M0), label=name)
ax[0].axvline(0.5, color="gray", ls=":"); ax[0].text(0.55, 0.05, "short TR\n(T1-weighting)")
ax[0].set(xlabel="TR [s]", ylabel="signal (TE = 0)", title="$T_1$ recovery: choose TR")
ax[1].axvline(100, color="gray", ls=":"); ax[1].text(105, 0.8, "long TE\n(T2-weighting)")
ax[1].set(xlabel="TE [ms]", ylabel="signal (TR = 10 s)", title="$T_2$ decay: choose TE")
for a in ax: a.legend(fontsize=9)
plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()

<<<md>>>
*Interpretation:*

* **Left (TR):** at short TR (~0.5 s) fat and white matter have recovered much more than CSF → large differences due
  to $T_1$. At very long TR all tissues have fully recovered and only $M_0$ differs.
* **Right (TE):** at very short TE nothing has decayed yet (only $M_0$ differs); at long TE (~100 ms) CSF ($T_2$ = 2 s)
  is still bright while tissues with $T_2$ ≈ 80 ms have lost most of their signal → $T_2$ contrast.

This gives the three classic recipes:

| Weighting | TR | TE | Bright | Dark |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **T1-weighted** | short (≈ $T_1$, 400–700 ms) | short (< 20 ms) | short $T_1$: fat, white matter, Gd-enhanced tissue | long $T_1$: CSF, edema |
| **T2-weighted** | long (≥ 3 $T_1$, 3–6 s) | long (≈ $T_2$, 80–120 ms) | long $T_2$: CSF, edema, many lesions | short $T_2$ |
| **PD-weighted** | long (3–6 s) | short (< 20 ms) | high proton density | low proton density |

(A short-TR **and** long-TE scan mixes both effects, which partly cancel — rarely useful.)

Let's make the three images from the phantom maps:

<<<code>>>
protocols = {"T1-weighted\nTR 500 ms, TE 15 ms": (0.015, 0.5),
             "T2-weighted\nTR 4000 ms, TE 100 ms": (0.100, 4.0),
             "PD-weighted\nTR 4000 ms, TE 15 ms": (0.015, 4.0)}
fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(12, 4.3))
for a, (title, (TE_, TR_)) in zip(ax, protocols.items()):
    S = mt.spin_echo_signal(TE_, TR_, ph["T1"], ph["T2"], ph["M0"])
    mt.show_image(S, ax=a, title=title)
plt.tight_layout(); plt.show()

<<<md>>>
*Interpretation:* same "patient", three looks. T1w: CSF in the ventricles is black, fat ring bright, white matter
brighter than gray matter. T2w: CSF very bright, the small "lesions" (longer $T_2$) stand out. PDw: everything is
fairly bright and similar — mainly proton density differences.

How strong the contrast is between two tissues depends on both TE and TR. Here is the white matter–CSF contrast
$C = S_{WM} - S_{CSF}$ for all combinations:

<<<code>>>
TEg, TRg = np.meshgrid(np.linspace(0.005, 0.25, 120), np.linspace(0.1, 6, 120))
M0w, T1w, T2w = tissues["white matter"]; M0c, T1c, T2c = tissues["CSF"]
C = mt.spin_echo_signal(TEg, TRg, T1w, T2w, M0w) - mt.spin_echo_signal(TEg, TRg, T1c, T2c, M0c)
plt.figure(figsize=(7, 4.5))
im = plt.pcolormesh(TEg*1e3, TRg, C, cmap="RdBu_r", vmin=-0.6, vmax=0.6, shading="auto")
plt.colorbar(im, label="$S_{WM} - S_{CSF}$")
plt.contour(TEg*1e3, TRg, C, levels=[0], colors="k")
for name, (TE_, TR_) in zip(["T1w", "T2w", "PDw"], protocols.values()):
    plt.plot(TE_*1e3, TR_, "ko"); plt.text(TE_*1e3 + 4, TR_ + 0.1, name)
plt.xlabel("TE [ms]"); plt.ylabel("TR [s]"); plt.title("White matter vs CSF contrast (red: WM brighter)"); plt.show()

<<<md>>>
*Interpretation:* red = white matter brighter (T1-like), blue = CSF brighter (T2-like). The black line is where the two
tissues have *identical* signal — a poorly chosen protocol could make the ventricles invisible!

